-*   **Precision:** Summing squared norms can lead to large values for Gaussians covered by many pixels. Ensure floating-point precision is sufficient to distinguish between high-error and low-error Gaussians.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### DS Threshold (DSTH)
-A hyperparameter threshold that controls the densification constraints of Gaussians. Increasing the threshold imposes stricter constraints, reducing the number of Gaussians (NGS), rendering quality, and training time [S3, unofficial]. The default value is 30 [S3, unofficial]. For the playroom scene in the DeepBlending dataset, it is set to 60; other scenes use 30 [S3, unofficial].
+A hyperparameter threshold that controls the densification constraints of Gaussians in the context of the Multi-View Density Control Criterion. Increasing DSTH imposes stricter constraints, which reduces the final number of Gaussians (NGS), decreases rendering quality, and shortens training time [S3, unofficial]. The default value is 30, but it is set to 60 for the "playroom" scene in the DeepBlending dataset, while other scenes use 30 [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(P)$, where $P$ is the total number of pixel-Gaussian intersections across all views. The norm calculation is constant time per intersection.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(G)$, where $G$ is the number of Gaussians, to store the error scores.
-*   **Performance:** On the "garden" scene, the multi-view criterion yields 27.06 dB PSNR and 0.1247 LPIPS using 148,658 primitives, compared to 26.83 dB PSNR and 0.1303 LPIPS using 240,075 primitives with the standard positional-gradient rule [S2, unofficial]. On average, it provides a 0.67 dB PSNR improvement and 0.028 LPIPS improvement while using 28% fewer primitives [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Resolves the nullification of gradients in multi-view settings; reduces primitive count without sacrificing quality.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires computing squared norms instead of simple vector addition; performance gains are specific to multi-view training regimes.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The computation requires iterating over all pixels in all views and accumulating squared norms for contributing Gaussians. This is $O(P \cdot N)$, where $P$ is the average number of pixels per view and $N$ is the number of views. This is comparable to the standard ADC cost but avoids the overhead of vector addition and potential normalization checks.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the accumulated error metric $E_1(G)$ per Gaussian, which is $O(|\mathcal{G}|)$, same as standard ADC.
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the "garden" scene, the Multi-View criterion yields 0.67 dB higher PSNR (27.06 vs 26.83) and 0.028 lower LPIPS (0.1247 vs 0.1303) than the standard positional-gradient rule, while using 28% fewer primitives (148,658 vs 240,075) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Resolves the invalid arithmetic issue in multi-view training; produces more compact representations (fewer Gaussians) with higher fidelity.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires multi-view training setup; the metric is specific to positional gradients and may need adaptation for other loss components.
+*   **Usage:** Applied during the teacher training phase to control primitive count and distribution [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed to address incompatibilities between standard 3D Gaussian Splatting densification and efficient multi-view training frameworks [S1, unofficial]. Standard ADC, which sums 2D positional gradients, fails in multi-view contexts because gradients from different image planes reside in different coordinate spaces [S1, unofficial]. The authors introduced this space-independent error metric to enable efficient multi-view training with reasonable overhead [S1, unofficial]. It is applied during the teacher training phase to control primitive count and distribution [S2, unofficial].
+The Multi-View Density Control Criterion was introduced in "Efficient multi-view training for 3D Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial] to address the incompatibility of the original 3DGS densification method with multi-view training. The original method summed 2D positional gradients, which is invalid when gradients come from different image planes [S1, unofficial]. The criterion was later referenced in "Bake It Till You Make It: Ultrafast Spatial Texture-Atlas Splatting" [S2, unofficial] as a superior alternative to standard positional-gradient rules for controlling primitive count.
 
